  "title": "The State of AI Financing: A Pause, Not a Pullback",
  "type": "credit",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-10-07",
  "insights": [
   "AI-related debt issuance slowed in September to ~$23bn globally, the second-lowest month this year, which we view as a temporary pause rather than a fundamental pullback.",
   "The slowdown is attributed to significant front-loading of issuance earlier in the year, emerging regulatory/political constraints on data center construction, and a rates sell-off impacting project-level deals.",
   "We expect a busier fall and 4Q26 as the focus shifts to 2027 capex, though we have lowered our 2026 ABS/CMBS issuance estimates to $25-30bn.",
   "In US IG, the macro backdrop is the primary test for credit markets through year-end, with potential for mutual fund/ETF outflows following a sharp drawdown in returns.",
   "Hyperscalers remain largely insensitive to higher rates due to strong ROIC visibility, and we expect another wave of volatility into 3Q26 earnings with likely further upward capex revisions.",
   "We reiterate our preference for collateral-backed risk over unsecured corporate risk, specifically favoring colocation over hyperscaler data center ABS.",
   "Non-USD hyperscaler issuance has broadened significantly, reaching $72bn YTD, with issuers tapping CAD, GBP, CHF, AUD, and JPY alongside EUR.",
