  "published": "2026-10-07",
  "insights": [
   "China's export share of global nominal goods rose from 4% in 2000 to 15% in 2025, surpassing historical peaks of Germany and Japan.",
   "Unlike 'China Shock 1.0' (2001-07), which was demand-driven and accompanied by industrial profit growth, the current 'China Shock 2.0' (since 2021) is supply-driven with stagnating or falling profitability.",
   "Auto industry profit margins fell from 6.4% in 2021 to 4.8% in 2025, despite a near-doubling of export value, highlighting massive production capacity.",
   "The number of countries running trade deficits with China exceeding 3% of GDP rose from 24 in 2005 to 82 in 2025, with 67 being low-income economies.",
   "Trading partners are substituting imports from non-China sources with Chinese goods, though the offset is less than one-for-one, leading to weaker balance-of-payments positions.",
   "Benchmarking analysis suggests China's share of global non-mineral imports could rise from 17.6% in 2024 to between 19.2% and 27.6% in the coming years.",
   "This potential market share gain implies continued robust real export growth of approximately 8% annually in the near term, provided importing economies can absorb the supply.",
   "Long-term sustainability of Chinese export growth depends on stronger economic growth in importing nations and managing balance-of-payments constraints in low-income EM countries."
